{"id": "a56199b8-680d-4fd7-a184-2199edcf17c1", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This requires many data-gathering calls across Cost Explorer, volumes, modifications, and DLM, so I'm considering delegating to a sub-agent since these are read-only operations. It's more of a "what changed" bounded troubleshooting path than an open-ended incident hunt, so I'll have the sub-agent follow the skill's defined procedure.

I'll need to include the full procedure directly in the prompt since the sub-agent may not have access to my skill, and specify the relevant account and region (Cost Explorer runs out of us-east-1) along with instructions to check associations. For the date range, since today is November 11, 2026, I'll have the sub-agent compare October against September and also pull November month-to-date figures.

"}]}

{"id": "2a682bef-291f-451b-9b43-79e0af0fd4ba", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll pull your EBS cost breakdown and the volume and snapshot change records to see what moved. That means a lot of Cost Explorer and EC2 data, so I'm running the analysis in the background.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_wCJNRZHw4D3ZYXkqVGvapW", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-bill-analysis", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
"task": "Explain why the user's Amazon EBS bill increased this month and identify what changed (component, resource, date). Read-only analysis only.",
"context": "User says 'My EBS bill jumped this month and I don't know why. What changed?' Current date is 2026-11-11 (November is month-to-date, so compare like-for-like: e.g. daily run-rate in Nov vs Oct, plus full-month Oct vs Sep and earlier months). Agent space account is 012345678901_1 (us-east-1); use list_associations to find other associated AWS accounts and check them too if present. EBS charges appear under the 'EC2 - Other' service in Cost Explorer (Cost Explorer endpoint is us-east-1).",
"procedure": [
 "1. Cost Explorer GetCostAndUsage: monthly for the last ~6 months, then daily for Oct-Nov, filtered to SERVICE 'EC2 - Other', grouped by USAGE_TYPE (also try grouping by LINKED_ACCOUNT / REGION if multi-account). Use GetDimensionValues for USAGE_TYPE to list the account's usage types; classify EBS ones by name: VolumeUsage (storage), IOPS (provisioned IOPS), Throughput (provisioned throughput), Snapshot (snapshots). Ignore non-EBS usage types (NAT, data transfer, etc.).",
 "2. Break the change into components (storage, IOPS, throughput, snapshots) using the account's own Cost Explorer amounts (never list prices). Identify the day the daily cost stepped up.",
 "3. Find what changed, in every region with cost: ec2 DescribeVolumesModifications (original/target size, type, IOPS, throughput, start time), DescribeVolumes (CreateTime for new volumes around the step-up date; status=available for unattached/idle volumes; volume types, sizes, IOPS, throughput), DescribeSnapshots (OwnerIds=self; count/size/StartTime growth, snapshots created recently), DLM GetLifecyclePolicies and AWS Backup ListBackupPlans for policies creating/retaining snapshots, Recycle Bin ListRules (resources in Recycle Bin still billed). Note: gp3 includes 3000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s; only above that is billed.",
 "4. Optionally try CloudTrail via lookup_cloudtrail_events (read the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill first) to find who made the key change; if denied/empty, record as a Gap and continue.",
 "5. Right-sizing check for volumes with provisioned IOPS/throughput above baseline: compare measured IOPS/MiB/s from CloudWatch AWS/EBS (VolumeReadOps+VolumeWriteOps etc. Sum math) against provisioned values. Keep this brief."
],
"output_format": "Return concise report with: (1) Answer: which component/resource drove the increase, with dollar amounts from Cost Explorer for the compared periods and the date; (2) Evidence table by component (storage, IOPS, throughput, snapshots) for the two periods; (3) change records explaining each (volume IDs, modification start times, CreateTimes, snapshot counts); (4) idle/unattached/over-provisioned candidates with volume IDs and exact parameters, noting cost direction (proposals only, no changes); (5) Gaps: any API that was denied/unavailable. Keep it concise. Do not make any mutating calls.",
"criteria": "Every number must come from an API call in this session, with units and time window. Snapshot savings are an upper bound since snapshots are incremental."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze EBS cost trend and recent volume/snapshot changes"}], "created_at": null}

